s\".\n\n### 3) Assumptions & uncertainties — marked load-bearing vs routine\n\n**Load-bearing (could meaningfully change the answer — researcher input recommended):**\n- **L1. Per-configuration sample counts & imbalance** in DrivAerNet++'s parametric subset. If notchback >> estateback, \"improvement with N\" may be dominated by the smallest class. Need to fix a stratification policy that doesn't artificially inflate any class.\n- **L2. Train-fraction grid exact values.** Different grids change curve shape and saturation point. A standardised 5-point log-spaced grid is proposed.\n- **L3. Number of repeats R.** Too few → noisy comparisons; too many → compute blow-up. R = 5 proposed as default.\n- **L4. Hyperparameter protocol.** Whether each architecture gets a **short** tuned setup or a **fixed default**. Choice biases the \"architecture comparison\" hugely; MLP especially varies with epochs/lr. Proposal: **one short, equal-cost random search per family (~10 trials)** to avoid hand-favouring any.\n- **L5. Selection of test/train split ratio and seed.** Affects absolute numbers; curves remain comparable internally.\n- **L6. Installation / availability of ML libraries** (currently not installed; network disabled). Proposal: prefer `scikit-learn` + `numpy` + `scipy` only (CPU-only, pip-installable offline if needed), include XGBoost/LightGBM only if already present to avoid installation issues.\n\n**Routine (low-risk, but worth recording):**\n- R1. Random seeds list (e.g., 0–4).\n- R2. Error bars: 95% CI vs ±1 std.\n- R3. Plot styling.\n- R4. Whether to shuffle inside the train pool.\n- R5. File naming convention of outputs.\n\n**Cross-cutting unknown (affects almost every load-bearing item):**\n- **U1.** The actual DrivAerNet++ release ingested: parameter list, units, total sample count, configuration labels, and whether a precomputed parametric subset exists or whether the 26 features must be re-derived from the mesh/geometry. The whole experiment plan grid (especially L1, L2, L5) depends on this. **Without this, the design carries assumptions; with it, all assumptions become facts.**\n\nI am **not** ready to commit a full design yet because L1–L6 and U1 are open. Please indicate (a) whether class balance / sample counts are known to you, (b) preferred architecture families, (c) exact train-fraction grid and number of repeats acceptable, and (d) any hyperparameter protocol preference, so the full design can be locked.","evidence":"Concrete findings shared with researcher:\n- Runtime check: `python3 --version` → Python 3.12.3; `nproc` → 24 CPUs; `uname` → x86_64 Linux. No GPU exposed.\n- Workspace listing: `/workspace` contains exactly one file, `task.json`, 752 bytes. No subdirectories.\n- File search: `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname 'track.md'` returned no matches; `find / -maxdepth 4 -type d -iname '*drivaer*'` returned no matches. No DrivAerNet++ data files are present on disk anywhere in the visible filesystem.\n- Library check: `import` of numpy, scipy, sklearn, pandas, matplotlib, torch, xgboost, lightgbm all failed with `ModuleNotFoundError`. `pip list` shows only stock system packages (cloud-init, boto3, etc.) — no ML Python packages.\n- task.json contents reproduced above: confirms the brief itself, that this is design-only (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"), and that the only doc reference is the literal string about `track.md`, not an actual file.\n- All \"26 parameters / fastback+notchback+estateback / drag\" specifics were taken verbatim from the researcher's message and the task.json question; nothing about them was invented or sourced from local files.\n- No claim is made that any experiment has been run; no fictional numbers, splits, or R² values appear above.","assumptions":"[-]","uncertainties":["Per-configuration sample counts and imbalance in DrivAerNet++'s parametric subset (load-bearing).","Exact 26 parameter names, units, and ranges (not documented in /workspace).","Whether the p